{#- _partials/_dedup.py.j2 — the per-pipeline deduplication drop (p64 R7.1/R7.2).

    WHAT IT EMITS. One `dropDuplicates` call on `df_transformed`, and nothing
    else. When `quality_config.dedup_columns` is non-empty the columns are
    interpolated as a Python list literal; when it is empty the call is made with
    NO argument, which is Spark's own "every column" behaviour and is therefore
    R7.2's default expressed directly rather than through a sentinel. p64 2c.2
    settled that `[]` and an omitted `dedup_columns` mean the same thing, so
    `code_assembly.resolve_quality_config` normalises both to `[]` and this
    partial needs only the one test.

    OFF BY DEFAULT, AND OFF MEANS ZERO RENDERED BYTES. R7.1 requires that no
    existing pipeline's behaviour changes, so the disabled arm emits nothing at
    all — not even a banner. That is stronger than it needs to be for
    *behaviour* (a comment changes none) and it is deliberate: it keeps the
    rendered job BYTE-IDENTICAL for every pipeline that has not opted in, which
    is a property a test can assert cheaply over the whole render matrix
    (`test_dedup_all_targets.py`'s
    `test_the_disabled_render_is_byte_identical_to_the_pre_dedup_tree`). The DQ
    partial beside it took the other option — its banner renders unconditionally
    — because 2b.1 was a pure extraction that had to reproduce P28's existing
    output line for line. This is a new block, so it has no such obligation.

    WHITESPACE IS LOAD-BEARING HERE, which is unusual enough to say out loud, and
    two separate pieces of it are.

    (1) THE ZERO-BYTE DISABLED ARM. The caller's `{%- include %}` strips the
    newline that ended the line above it, and this file's outer `{%- if %}` then
    restores exactly one via the blank line that opens its body, while
    `{%- endif -%}` strips the trailing one. Disabled, the two cancel to nothing.
    Do not "tidy" either side's minus signs independently — they are one
    expression, and the byte-identity test named above is what catches it.

    (2) THE `dropDuplicates` LINES SHARE A LINE WITH THEIR `{% if %}` TAG, and
    that is not a formatting slip. The render environment leaves `trim_blocks`
    off, so a tag on its own line contributes the newline that follows it to the
    branch body — which would emit a blank line between the banner and the call.
    The obvious fix, closing the tag with the whitespace-stripping form, strips the
    newline AND the four spaces of indentation behind it, producing
    `df_transformed = …` at column 0 inside the job's `try:` and a job that does
    not compile. That was written first and caught by `ast.parse` on the rendered
    output, so the same-line form is the third option and the only correct one.

    WHY THE EMPTY-COLUMN CASE IS A SEPARATE BRANCH RATHER THAN AN EMPTY LIST.
    `dropDuplicates([])` is NOT `dropDuplicates()`: PySpark passes the subset
    straight through to the JVM, so an empty subset deduplicates on zero columns
    and collapses the entire frame to a single row. Silent, catastrophic, and
    indistinguishable from a working job until someone counts the target. Emitting
    the no-argument call is the only correct rendering of "all columns", which is
    why `resolve_quality_config`'s `[]` must never reach the argument position.

    WHERE THE CALLER MUST PUT IT: immediately BEFORE the DQ 'evaluate' include
    site, i.e. after the transform and after any auto-cast/column selection, and
    before `transformed_count`. That ordering is a decision, not a convenience,
    and both halves of it matter:

      * BEFORE the DQ split. `_generate_dqdl_rules` can emit
        `Uniqueness "f" >= 0.95`, so duplicate rows can fail a DQDL rule. Placed
        AFTER the split, those rows would be quarantined as quality failures
        before dedup ever saw them: `rows_quarantined` would absorb the dedup
        drops and the two row-loss causes would be reported as one. R7.6 forbids
        exactly that ("`rows_read - rows_written` would attribute deduplication
        to quarantine or to the transform"), and 2c.4 needs the two separable.
        Placed here, a count either side of this block brackets the dedup drops
        and nothing else.
      * AFTER the column selection. R7.2's default is "all mapped target
        columns", and after the select `df_transformed` carries exactly those —
        so a bare `dropDuplicates()` IS that default. Before the select the frame
        may still carry source-only columns, and two rows identical in every
        target column but differing in a dropped one would both survive, leaving
        duplicates in the target. That is R7.2 wrong rather than merely
        different.
      * NOT after `transformed_count`. That count is published as the DQ report's
        `rows_passed` and as `_load_counts`' `rows_written`, so deduplicating
        after it would make both describe a frame larger than the one written —
        reintroducing the row-loss lie `shared/load_outcome.py` exists to remove.

    THE PRECISE CLAIM ABOUT THE EMPTY-LIST DEFAULT, because "all mapped target
    columns" is true of three templates and approximately true of two. The bare
    call keys on every column of the frame that is about to be written, which is
    the honest and useful reading on all five. It coincides with the mapped
    target columns wherever a select has run: `glue_job_s3_tables.py.j2`, and
    `glue_job_glue_catalog.py.j2` / `glue_job_jdbc_source.py.j2` when their
    selects succeed — both of those sit in a `try` that logs and skips when the
    target table does not exist yet, so on a first run they may not have.
    `glue_job_jdbc.py.j2` and `glue_job_s3_parquet.py.j2` have no select block at
    all, so the frame is whatever the transform produced. In every one of those
    cases the key is "the columns being written", and that is what an operator
    who left the column list empty asked for.

    REQUIRED CONTEXT VARIABLE: `quality_config`, assigned unconditionally in
    `code_assembly.generate_code`'s `template_vars` from
    `resolve_quality_config` (R6.5 / do-NOT 6, design C7). That function returns
    BOTH recognised keys on every call, which is what lets the reads below be
    plain `quality_config.dedup_enabled` / `.dedup_columns` rather than reaching
    for the default-value filter — under StrictUndefined a dict access to an
    absent key yields an `Undefined` whose `__bool__` raises, so the keys being
    filled upstream is the whole mechanism. do-NOT 6 forbids that filter here for
    the reason `_partials/_load_counts.py.j2`'s header records, and
    `test_dq_template_vars_contract.py`'s
    `test_the_p64_partials_read_nothing_through_default` counts its occurrences in
    this file at zero.

    THE OUTER GUARD IS `is mapping and … is true`, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY **NOT**
    THE `is defined and` IDIOM `dqdl_rules` AND `data_quality_enabled` USE. That
    was the first draft and it FAILED an existing test, which is worth recording
    because the sibling partial's header reasons the hazard away:
    `_data_quality.py.j2` says `is defined`'s two-jinja2-copies exposure was
    "analysed and dismissed — `is defined` only consults sentinel identity for an
    UNSET variable, and `code_assembly.generate_code` sets this one
    unconditionally on every production render". Both halves of that are true and
    the conclusion still does not carry here, because the render sites that omit
    the variable are exactly the ones under test:
    `test_dq_template_vars_contract.py`'s
    `test_dq_block_survives_a_second_jinja2_copy_in_sys_modules` renders all five
    templates with no `quality_config`, and with two jinja2 copies loaded
    `Context.resolve_or_missing` hands back copy A's `missing` sentinel while the
    compiled template tests identity against copy B's. `_MissingType` is not an
    `Undefined`, so `is defined` answered **True**, and the attribute read behind
    it then raised `UndefinedError: 'dedup_enabled' is undefined` — five failures,
    on the very test that exists to catch this class of thing. Loud rather than
    silent, so it was safe; it was still wrong.

    `is mapping` consults no sentinel at all — it is an `isinstance` check against
    `abc.Mapping`, which a `_MissingType`, an `Undefined` and a stray scalar all
    fail — so the guard's answer is independent of how many jinja2 copies are
    loaded. That is the property the sibling idiom lacks. It also fails CLOSED in
    every case: unset, `None`, a scalar, or a dict without the key all disable
    dedup, and nothing enables it by accident. `.get(...)` rather than attribute
    access for the same reason, and it is NOT the filter do-NOT 6 forbids: that
    filter decides by testing whether its input is an `Undefined` instance, which
    is the identity question two jinja2 copies make unanswerable, whereas `.get`
    is a plain method on a value `is mapping` has already established is a real
    dict. The filter's name is deliberately not spelled anywhere in this file —
    the fence over it is a substring count across the whole file, so a header that
    named it would be counted as a violation of itself, which is
    `LGR-b46a5bb-4`'s lesson and cost this very paragraph one test failure before
    it was reworded. `_data_quality.py.j2`'s header carries the same note for the
    same reason.

    `is true` rather than plain truthiness mirrors `resolve_quality_config`'s own
    rule that only an explicit `bool` opts in, so the string `"false"` cannot mean
    enabled here either. Defence in depth: that resolver already normalises, and
    the API validator already refuses, so this is the third layer and it costs one
    word.

    The loudness a `is defined`-free guard gives up is recovered upstream and
    mechanically, by
    `test_dq_template_vars_contract.py`'s
    `test_every_p64_partial_variable_is_set_unconditionally_in_template_vars`,
    which reads this partial's variable surface and fails if `quality_config` is
    ever missing from `template_vars`, assigned inside an `if`, or routed through
    the source_config/target_config splat.

    THE COLUMN LIST IS EMITTED THROUGH `tojson`, NEVER BY INTERPOLATION — the
    same rule `column_rename_block` follows (do-NOT 17). Every entry has already
    passed the identifier allowlist twice, at the API boundary
    (`pipelines_crud._validate_quality_config`, which REFUSES rather than
    filters) and again at render time (`resolve_quality_config`'s
    `validate_identifiers` call on the `_DEDUP_COLUMN_SINK`), so nothing hostile
    can reach here. `tojson` is the third layer and costs nothing: it emits a
    quoted, escaped list literal, so even a name that somehow bypassed both
    allowlists becomes a Python string rather than syntax.

    THE DROP IS BRACKETED BY ITS OWN TWO COUNTS (p64 R7.6, task 2c.4), AND THAT
    COSTS TWO EXTRA SPARK ACTIONS. Both are paid ONLY inside this enabled arm, so
    a pipeline that has not opted in pays nothing and the disabled arm stays at
    zero rendered bytes. `_adi_dedup_dropped` is the local the number travels in;
    `_partials/_load_counts.py.j2` reads it and puts it on the
    `(run_id, "load_metrics")` state row as `rows_deduplicated`.

    TWO, not one, and NEITHER existing count can be reused — which is worth
    stating because "reuse a count" is the obvious first idea and it is wrong on
    both sides:

      * `source_count` is PRE-TRANSFORM. The transform sits between it and this
        block, so `source_count - <post-drop>` folds transform drops into the
        dedup number.
      * `transformed_count` is POST-QUARANTINE, not post-dedup. The DQ 'evaluate'
        stage reassigns the frame — `df_transformed = _dq_passed_df` in
        `_partials/_data_quality.py.j2` — and that site sits between this block
        and `transformed_count`. So `<pre-drop> - transformed_count` folds
        QUARANTINE drops into the dedup number, which is R7.6's own defect with
        the labels swapped: the requirement exists because
        `rows_read - rows_written` "would attribute deduplication to quarantine or
        to the transform", and that formulation would attribute quarantine to
        deduplication instead. Trading one misattribution for its mirror image is
        not a saving.

    So the honest bracket is a count immediately before the drop and a count
    immediately after it, both here, where nothing else can get between them.
    The subtraction is left unwrapped: `count()` returns a Python `int`, and the
    explicit `int(...)` that `LGR-9d1c0e4-1` requires belongs at the DynamoDB
    value site, which is in `_load_counts.py.j2` — the same division that partial
    already applies to `source_count`.

    NO STAGE VARIABLE. `_data_quality.py.j2` and `_load_counts.py.j2` take one
    because they have several ordered emission points that read each other's
    locals; this partial has exactly one, so a stage argument would be ceremony
    with a second failure mode. `_runtime_args.py.j2` and the four `_write_*`
    partials are the existing single-purpose precedent in this directory.

    ALL of the rationale lives in this Jinja comment and none of it in the
    emitted Python, per `LGR-b46a5bb-4` (template / ratified): this repo's
    template fences are substring assertions over the RENDERED output, so naming
    a fenced token in emitted Python would be counted as an occurrence of it.
-#}
{%- if quality_config is mapping and quality_config.get("dedup_enabled") is true %}

    # === Deduplication (p64 R7.1/R7.2) ===
    # p64 R7.6: bracket the drop with its own counts so the rows it removes are
    # attributed to deduplication and not to the transform or the quarantine.
    # Two extra Spark actions, paid only by a pipeline that opted in.
    _adi_dedup_rows_before = df_transformed.count()
{% if quality_config.get("dedup_columns") %}    df_transformed = df_transformed.dropDuplicates({{ quality_config.get("dedup_columns") | tojson }})
{%- else %}    df_transformed = df_transformed.dropDuplicates()
{%- endif %}
    _adi_dedup_dropped = _adi_dedup_rows_before - df_transformed.count()
    logger.info(f"Deduplication dropped {_adi_dedup_dropped} duplicate rows")
    # === End Deduplication ===
{%- endif -%}
